<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Hris.Foundation.Notification
    Sprint 5 (Workflow Engine and Notification Framework) scope, per IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md
    Phase 1: second and last of Sprint 5's own two frameworks, built as a genuine mutual
    dependency cycle with Workflow Engine but as its own separate PR, the same
    "one framework, one PR, even within one Sprint" discipline every earlier
    multi-framework Sprint already establishes. Source specification:
    docs/03-foundation/notification-framework.md, built entirely from scratch this
    Sprint: no Domain layer existed for this one before this pass.

    Deliberately narrower than the source document's own full Scope, the same "records
    the configuration, does not build the runtime that walks it" split every Sprint 4/5
    config-plus-occurrence framework already draws for its own out-of-scope runtime
    concern:

    - No template-variable substitution/rendering engine. NotificationTemplateVersion.Body
      and Notification.Body both carry already-resolved or opaque placeholder-bearing
      text; the publishing module or a future rendering step substitutes
      {{Variable}}-style placeholders before this framework's own aggregates see the
      content.
    - No delivery worker that actually sends through Email/SMS/push/Slack/Teams/webhook
      provider integrations, applies user preferences and quiet hours, or fans a
      request out across a recipient's own preferred channels. This build records
      requests, templates, and preferences, and exposes lifecycle-transition commands a
      future worker calls: it does not implement that worker itself, the identical
      "does not build the actual worker process" scope boundary Job Processing
      Framework's own remarks already draw for itself.
    - No actual quiet-hours/digest-mode scheduling logic: NotificationPreference
      records the settings; applying them is this document's own stated worker
      responsibility ("Apply user preferences and quiet hours in the worker, not in the
      publishing module"), a real operational concern this Sprint does not attempt.

    This Sprint builds what the document's own AI Implementation Guidance actually names
    as non-negotiable: asynchronous delivery with no business operation ever waiting on
    it, never rolling back a committed business action because delivery failed, retry
    with backoff routing exhausted attempts to a dead letter state, per-recipient-and-
    per-channel delivery tracking, explicit tenant-scoped recipient resolution, and the
    two client-facing endpoints (GetMyNotificationsQuery, MarkNotificationReadCommand)
    this document itself already fully specifies: both strictly self-scoped, with no
    admin-override path and no client-facing CreateNotificationCommand.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Hris.Foundation.Notification</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="MediatR" Version="12.4.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentValidation" Version="11.11.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentValidation.DependencyInjectionExtensions" Version="11.11.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" Version="9.0.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="9.0.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Hris.SharedKernel\Hris.SharedKernel.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Hris.Application\Hris.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Hris.Infrastructure\Hris.Infrastructure.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
